---
/* THE PAGE THE FRONT PAGE KEPT FOLDING AWAY.
 *
 * Two panels of prose — why the atlas is a map, and what it does not know —
 * sat under the map behind a <details>. Folding them was the right call for
 * the front page and the wrong home for the text: a reader who has already
 * got the point scrolled past it every visit, and a reader who arrived with a
 * surname met an essay before they met an answer.
 *
 * So the text lives here, on a page somebody reaches by asking for help, and
 * it is allowed to be longer than a fold. The front page keeps the map.
 *
 * WHILE MOVING IT, TWO THINGS WERE WRONG AND ARE FIXED:
 *   · «Online is four different things» — it is eight, and the eighth is
 *     «nobody has looked», which is nine dots in ten.
 *   · The counts printed as 21473 and 2547650, unseparated, because the front
 *     page interpolated them raw.
 * Every number here is counted at build time.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { atlas as idx } from "../lib/atlas.js";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";
import provs from "../../public/providers.json";

const places = idx.places.length;
const volumes = idx.places.reduce((a, p) => a + (p.v || 0), 0);
const archives = provs.providers.filter((p) => p.at);
const archiveCountries = new Set(archives.map((p) => p.at.place.split(", ").pop())).size;

const RANK = idx.rank || [];
const SURVEYED = new Set(["free", "account", "index", "mixed", "paid", "catalogue", "onsite"]);
const accessOf = (p) => (typeof p.a === "number" && RANK[p.a]) || "unsurveyed";
const surveyed = idx.places.filter((p) => SURVEYED.has(accessOf(p))).length;
const unsurveyedPct = Math.round((1 - surveyed / places) * 1000) / 10;

/* The access vocabulary, read from the index rather than restated, so this
   page cannot drift from the colours the map actually draws. */
const access = idx.access || {};
---
<Base
  title="Help"
  eyebrow="How to search it · what the colours mean · what to do when it finds nothing"
  dek="Most of what a reader needs is one of three things: how to find a place whose name has changed, what the colour of a dot is telling them, and what to do when the answer is nothing.">

  <div class="prose" style="max-width:74ch">
    <h3>Three ways to search</h3>
    <p><b>A surname.</b> Type it and the map lights the countries a register or
    an archive attests it in. It will not tell you your family lived there — it
    tells you the name is recorded there, which is where to start.</p>
    <p><b>A village, by any name it has ever had.</b> This is the one most
    people need and do not know to ask for. Type <em>Gallignana</em> and the map
    goes to Gračišće; <em>Breslau</em> answers Wrocław, <em>Czernowitz</em>
    answers Chernivtsi, <em>Memel</em> answers Klaipėda. It works worldwide, not
    only where this atlas is deep.</p>
    <p><b>A name and a place together.</b> <em>Blazevic Gracisce</em> narrows to
    that name on that ground.</p>
    <p><b>Or just click a country.</b> Every country on earth answers with
    something — whose national archive, what research guide exists — even where
    this atlas holds nothing below the country itself.</p>

    <h3>Why a map, and not another list</h3>
    <p><b>Three things stand between a researcher and a record, and all three
    are geographic.</b></p>
    <p><b>The name changed.</b> You cannot search for a place whose name you do
    not know. That is why the find box matches every name a place has ever had
    rather than the one it happens to carry today.</p>
    <p><b>The jurisdiction changed.</b> An 1863 baptism from a Croatian village
    sits in a Croatian archive. A 1930 one, from the same village, is filed
    under <em>Italy</em> — because between the wars that village was Italian.
    Searching the Croatian collection alone finds nothing, and <b>nothing is not
    the same as not existing</b>. Set the year on the map and it tells you which
    it is; <a href={u("/jurisdictions/")}>the table behind that</a> is the part
    of this atlas nobody else publishes.</p>
    <p><b>"Online" is eight different things</b>, not one, and the difference is
    the whole cost of an evening. Here it is the colour of the dot:</p>
    <ul class="akey">
      {Object.entries(access).map(([k, v]) => (
        <li><b>{k}</b> — {v}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>The last of those is the commonest colour on this map, and the next
    section is about that.</p>

    <h3>When it finds nothing</h3>
    <p><b>A grey dot means nobody has surveyed it. It does not mean nothing is
    there.</b> That distinction is the difference between a map and a claim, and
    keeping it is worth more than coverage — a map that quietly implies
    completeness sends people home when they should keep looking.</p>
    <p>So if a place is grey, the useful next steps are, in order: set the year
    and see <b>whose archive held that ground</b>, because the records may be
    filed under a country you were not searching; open <a href={u("/providers/")}>the
    source directory</a> for that country and write to the archive named there;
    and check whether the ground was covered by a newspaper rather than a
    register — for some countries, Australia above all, the newspaper <em>is</em>
    the record.</p>
    <p>If you find the answer, <a href={u("/add/")}>send it back</a>. A grey dot
    you have personally turned into an answer is the single most valuable thing
    a stranger can give this map.</p>

    <h3>What this map does not know yet</h3>
    <p>It is honest about being early, and the layers are at different stages.
    <b>{unsurveyedPct}% of the ground here has never been surveyed</b> — that is
    the number every other one refines.</p>
    <p><b>The archives layer is worldwide</b> — {archives.length} institutions
    across {archiveCountries} countries, each saying what it holds and what it
    costs to look. <b>The shelf is deep in one corner</b>: {places.toLocaleString()}
    places and {volumes.toLocaleString()} volumes, with Croatia, Istria and
    Carnia far ahead of everywhere else. <b>The jurisdiction layer covers
    {regions.regions.length} pieces of ground</b>, not the planet —
    {" "}{regions.regions.slice(0, 8).map((r) => r.label.split(" — ")[0]).join(", ")}
    {regions.regions.length > 8 ? ` and ${regions.regions.length - 8} more` : ""}.
    A place outside them says so in its own panel rather than guessing, because
    a confident wrong answer about which archive to write to costs somebody a
    month.</p>

    <h3>Still stuck?</h3>
    <p><a href={u("/start/")}>Start here</a> walks the first search end to end.
    <a href={u("/about/")}>About</a> says what this atlas is and where it came
    from, and <a href={u("/coverage/")}>what's missing</a> measures the gaps
    country by country rather than asserting coverage.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .akey { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 10px 0 16px; display: grid;
          gap: 4px; }
  .akey li { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-2);
             line-height: 1.55; }
  .akey b { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--ink);
            text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; }
</style>
